    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d156">

                <pb facs="0594" n="565" sameAs="frus:frus1977-80v22/pg565"/>

                <head>156. Letter From President Carter to Burmese President Ne Win<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d156-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1977-06-15">June 15, 1977</date></dateline>
                    <salute>Dear Mr. President:</salute>
                </opener>
                <p>I want to express my special thanks and admiration for the deep commitment you
                    have made to deal with the narcotics problem.</p>
                <p>Following his recent visit to your country, Dr. Peter Bourne has told me not only
                    of your determination to deal with this problem, but also of the progress you
                    have already made against both the cultivation and the trafficking of
                        drugs.<anchor n="2" xml:id="d156-f2"/></p>
                <p>The entire world community joins in applauding your efforts.</p>
                <p>Sincerely,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed><hi rendition="#bold">Jimmy Carter</hi></signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d156-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d156-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Carter Library, National Security Affairs, Brzezinski Material,
                    President’s Correspondence with Foreign Leaders File, Box 2, Burma, President U
                    Ne Win, 6/77. No classification marking. Thai Prime Minister Thanin Kraiwichian
                    received an identical letter. (Carter Library, National Security Affairs,
                    Brzezinski Material, President’s Correspondence with Foreign Leaders File, Box
                    19, Thailand, Prime Minister Thanin Kraiwichian, 6/77)</p>
            </note>
